 | **Secondary** scale check (higher difficulty, asymmetric) |\n\nNo other SMAX variants are used, to keep compute bounded and the story focused.\n\n## 3. Methods\n\n### 3.1 PPO side: MAPPO (JaxMARL's built-in PPO)\n- JaxMARL's PPO implementation is **MAPPO**: mean-field PPO with a centralized value function, run on JaxMARL's vectorized parallel environments.\n- Config: 128 parallel envs (matched to QMIX), rollout length 128, 4 gradient epochs per rollout, library-default learning rate/hidden sizes (recorded from the installed package in the run log), default gamma/lam.\n\n### 3.2 Q-learning side: centralized QMIX-style DQN (chosen variant)\n- **Interpretation statement (not a confirmed source fact):** \"Q-Learning\" is interpreted as tabular Q-learning lifted to function approximation \u2014 i.e., a value-based, centralized-critic DQN family. The chosen instantiation is **QMIX** (value-decomposed per-agent Q-functions with a centralized mixing network; centralized training, decentralized execution).\n- **Rationale for QMIX over independent DQN:** (a) it is the canonical value-based MARL method in the SMAX literature; (b) it matches MAPPO's information access (centralized critic), so the comparison isolates *policy-gradient vs. value-based learning* rather than confounding it with credit-assignment architecture; (c) JaxMARL ships a QMIX implementation, so both methods run on identical infrastructure with the same vectorization.\n- **Sensitivity option (pre-registered, run only if compute remains after the main run):** independent DQN (one DQN per agent, no centralization) to bound the effect of the centralization choice.\n- Config: 128 parallel envs, replay buffer and network width matched to MAPPO's where directly comparable (recorded in run log).\n\n## 4. Metrics \u2014 precise definitions\n\n### 4.1 Normalized score (primary quality metric)\n- **Definition:** `NS = (S_mean \u2212 S_random) / (S_oracle \u2212 S_random)`\n- **S_random (self-contained, reproducible):** mean episode score of a uniform-random policy in the *identical* environment, measured with \u22652000 episodes (32 parallel evals \u00d7 63 batches), averaged over 5 seeds of the random policy itself; measured once per environment at setup and stored in the workspace.\n- **S_oracle:** the oracle-policy episode score for the map, **transcribed from the SMAX source paper \u2014 this is the single point where an external value must be entered** (one constant per map in `scoring.py`, with the paper citation and section in a comment). **Fallback (pre-registered, labeled in the report):** if the oracle value for a map cannot be verified, use `S_self = max` mean evaluation score achieved by any method/seed in the run as the reference, and report both NS values (oracle-based where available, self-based otherwise).\n- **Evaluation protocol (identical for both methods):** every 10k training steps, evaluate the deterministic policy for 32 episodes (fresh env, identical config), report the mean.\n\n### 4.2 Training time (efficiency)\n- **Primary: timesteps-to-target** \u2014 number of agent-steps to first reach NS \u2265 0.90 and NS \u2265 0.99 (recorded as \"not reached\" if the budget is exhausted; analyzed as censored observations).\n- **Secondary: wall-clock time** to the same targets, and wall-clock at the fixed 1M-step budget. Wall-clock is secondary because, on this CPU-only box, JaxMARL's vectorization advantage is GPU-dependent and CPU wall-clock is noisier.\n- **Measurement:** `time.perf_counter()` around the training loop only; JAX compilation is warmed up with a fixed pre-timing rollout; setup/env-creation excluded. Per-run CPU affinity pinned to 16 cores with `OMP_NUM_THREADS=16` (same for both methods) so wall-clock is comparable.\n\n## 5. Controls table\n\n| Control | Value | Applies to |\n|---|---|---|\n| Environment | `smax_3m3m` (primary), `smax_3m5m` (secondary); identical obs/action spaces, defaults otherwise | both methods |\n| Episode length | 20 steps (JaxMARL SMAX default) | both |\n| Timestep budget | 1,000,000 agent-steps per seed (fallback: 500k) | both |\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2, 3, 4} (fallback: {0, 1, 2}) | both |\n| Parallel-env count | 128 | both |\n| Evaluation cadence / episodes | every 10k steps / 32 episodes, deterministic | both |\n| CPU pinning | `taskset` to 16 cores, `OMP_NUM_THREADS=16`, `XLA_FLAGS=--xla_force_host_platform_device_count=1` | both |\n| JAX config | `jax_platform_name=cpu`, pinned jax/jaxmarl versions recorded in run log, Python 3.11\u20133.12 | both |\n| Normalization refs | S_random (measured, \u00a74.1), S_oracle (transcribed, \u00a74.1) | both |\n| Network width | matched where directly comparable; deviations logged | both |\n\n## 6. Procedure\n\n1. **Setup:** provision Python 3.11\u20133.12; install jax (CPU) + jaxmarl from pinned versions; verify `smax_3m3m`/`smax_3m5m` and the QMIX implementation exist (if QMIX is absent, implement compact in-house QMIX on JaxMARL's env API \u2014 pre-registered path); record all versions and library defaults in `run_log.md`.\n2. **Random baseline:** measure S_random per environment (\u00a74.1); store constant.\n3. **Smoke test (10 min, both methods, 50k steps each, 1 seed):** measure steps/s, memory, wall-clock stability. This is calibration only and is excluded from results.\n4. **Full run:** 2 methods \u00d7 5 seeds \u00d7 1M steps on `smax_3m3m`; then `smax_3m5m` with the same design.\n5. **Fallback ladder (pre-registered, applied in order after smoke test):** if smoke-test estimate of full-run cost exceeds the available budget, (a) drop to 3 seeds; (b) drop to 500k steps (targets re-expressed as NS \u2265 0.90 only); (c) drop the `smax_3m5m` secondary run. Every fallback taken is reported.\n\n## 7. Analysis plan\n\n- **Per-seed aggregation:** for each method, across the 5 seeds: mean \u00b1 95% CI (t-based, n=5) of final NS (at budget end) and of NS at each 100k-step checkpoint; timesteps-to-target reported as median + IQR with censored observations flagged.\n- **Statistically meaningful difference:**\n  - *Normalized score:* a difference is claimed only if the 95% CIs of final NS (or of NS at the step where the faster method first hit 0.90) do not overlap **and** a paired test across matched seeds (Wilcoxon signed-rank, n=5) gives p < 0.05; with n=5 we additionally report the effect size (rank-biserial r) and interpret non-significant results as \"no detectable difference,\" not \"equivalent.\"\n  - *Training time:* compare timesteps-to-target with the same paired test; for wall-clock, we report means/CIs but claim a difference only if the CIs of per-seed wall-clock **and** of the steps/s throughput (from smoke test and full runs) exclude each other, acknowledging CPU noise.\n- **Secondary `smax_3m5m` scale check:** interpreted as a *consistency check, not a separate hypothesis* \u2014 the primary conclusion (which method is faster/better, if any) is considered robust only if the ranking of methods by NS-at-budget and timesteps-to-target is the same (or directionally consistent) on both maps; a reversal on 3m5m is reported as scale-dependent behavior.\n\n## 8. Feasibility (CPU-only, no GPU)\n\n- **Why it's feasible without a GPU:** SMAX is a tiny grid world; per-step environment cost is small and JAX's XLA CPU backend handles the vectorized 128-env batch. The GPU argument for JaxMARL is about throughput ceiling, not viability \u2014 on CPU the comparison is still valid on the *primary* axis (timesteps), which is hardware-normalized.\n- **Expected cost (estimates, to be confirmed by the smoke test):** 1M steps of 3m3m \u2248 **0.5\u20133 CPU-hours per seed** (i.e., ~minutes of wall-clock per 10k-step eval slice; total wall-clock per seed ~10 min\u20131.5 h at 16 pinned cores). Main run: 2 methods \u00d7 5 seeds \u00d7 [0.5\u20133 h] \u2248 **5\u201330 CPU-hours** on 3m3m; secondary run adds \u2248 5\u201330 CPU-hours; smoke test \u2248 20 min. Total \u2248 **10\u201360 CPU-hours**, comfortably absorbable by a 224-core box even with 16-core pinning (parallelizable across seeds).\n- **Decision rule for scaling down:** after the smoke test, compute `T_est = (1M steps / measured steps/s) \u00d7 10 runs + eval overhead`. If `T_est` exceeds the available budget or exceeds ~24 h wall-clock even with all seeds parallelized, apply the fallback ladder (\u00a76.5) in order. The primary metric (timesteps-to-target) remains valid at any rung; only wall-clock claims degrade with fewer steps.\n\n## 9. Assumptions and uncertainties (final)\n\n- **Assumptions:** jaxmarl installs cleanly and bundles both MAPPO and QMIX (verify at setup; in-house QMIX is the fallback); Python 3.11\u20133.12 will be provisioned; SMAX-paper oracle values are transcribable (single transcription point in `scoring.py`); QMIX is the intended Q-learning variant (researcher-approved interpretation, not a source fact); CPU pinning makes wall-clock comparable.\n- **Uncertainties:** compute budget is unspecified (hence smoke test + fallback ladder); per-seed runtime is an unverified 0.5\u20133 h estimate; CPU wall-clock noise may make wall-clock-to-target cross between methods (mitigated by making timesteps primary); with n=5 seeds, statistical power is limited \u2014 non-significant results are reported as such; I did not have the referenced NeurIPS-2024 source sections in the sandbox, so exact library defaults are taken from the installed package and logged at setup.\n\n**No experiment was run. This is the complete design, with rationale, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility, for review and later execution.**", "summary": "Deliver final experiment design with controls, analysis plan, feasibility", "uncertainties": ["Per-seed runtime (0.5-3 CPU-hours for 1M steps) is an estimate pending the 10-minute smoke test; the pre-registered fallback ladder (3 seeds \u2192 500k steps \u2192 drop 3m5m) handles the case where it is wrong", "Statistical power at n=5 seeds is limited; the analysis plan therefore req